    an abstraction serving one real consumer.

  Which is expensive to change later: the transport choice and the log record shape (field names, level set), since
  anything consuming the logs depends on them. Which is cheap: log levels at individual call sites, and what's logged
  where.

  My recommendation is the browser app only. It's the code with real production failure modes, and scoping to it keeps
  the privacy work focused where the sensitive data actually is.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which program does "the app" mean for this logging work? → Browser app only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted — browser app only.

  Next question, and this is the one that drives the transport decision: a [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m is already there and invisible to
  you in production, so the question is what "debug production issues" means concretely today.

  If you're chasing reported failures after the fact, logs have to leave the user's browser and land somewhere you can
  query — that's a real subsystem (endpoint, batching, offline/failure handling, retention). If you mainly need better
  information when you're on a call with someone or reproducing locally, a structured in-browser logger with a level
  switch and a "dump the buffer" escape hatch gets you most of the way for a fraction of the work and no privacy
  exposure at all.
